      "writtenOn": "2025-03-21T15:57:09Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e If we just passed in colorAttachment-\u003edimensions() here, getDepthAttachmentDimensions() won\u0027t have to separately call GetApproxSize since it\u0027ll be GetApproxSize(U/V dims). Multiplying that by 2 to get the Y size almost always works out correctly. For every input size \u003e 8px, 2GetApproxSize(sz) \u003d\u003d GetApproxSize(2sz). For sz \u003c\u003d 8px, 2*GetApproxSize(sz) returns 32 instead of 16 but that shouldn\u0027t be problematic since that\u0027s still larger than the Y plane size.\n\nI don\u0027t think this will work. That will make the depth stencil texture double the size of the MSAA. We cannot use a depth/stencil attachment larger than a color attachment yet. We can make them both larger than the resolve texture. But both must match their size. Depth stencil\u0027s size \u003d 2 x sizeof(U/V) plane is a special rule in D3D11. The MSAA texture is not a U/V plane so that rule won\u0027t be applied.\n\nNevertheless I don\u0027t think the current code can handle the MSAA render to U/V plane correctly either. In D3D11, the resolve texture is not part of the render pass. So if we passed the MSAA attachment to the `getDepthAttachmentDimensions` it would return MSAA attachment\u0027s size x 2. When we bind the attachments in D3D11, it would complain because the MSAA attachment \u0026 depth stencil attachment have different size. The MSAA attachment is not a multiplanar plane, hence the special rule won\u0027t be applied.\n\nBut as you said, it\u0027s very unlikely that we render to a Y/U/V plane with AA. So I will just pass `colorAttachment-\u003edimensions()` to `getDepthAttachmentDimensions` for now.",
